// The one fixture that pins where `timeout`'s value comes from: the inner
// future's own output slot, not the timeout combinator's status slot.
//
// `answer()` never awaits anything, so it completes on `task_timeout_future`'s
// very first poll -- long before the 30-second deadline could ever matter.
// `timeout`'s status word is `0` (completed) either way; the only thing this
// distinguishes is which output slot `Ok(v)` reads.
//
// Writing `task_output` on the *timeout* future's own handle instead of
// `answer()`'s does not reach runtime here: Nova checks a generic function's
// body once, with its own type parameter held rigid, so `task_output`'s
// `Future<T>` parameter can never unify with the timeout future's concrete
// `Future<Int>` -- that mistake is rejected at compile time (`E0010`),
// regardless of what any call site instantiates `T` as. Measured directly
// (Task 4's report): the equivalent Nova-source mutation fails to compile,
// it does not run and print `0`.
//
// This fixture's discriminating power is one layer down from there, at the
// runtime/lowering boundary the type system cannot see into -- and it is
// narrower than two earlier versions of this comment claimed. Measured, by
// running each mutation rather than reasoning about it:
//
//   - A wrong slot layout in `poll_timeout` -- writing the combinator's status
//     into the *inner's* output slot as well as its own -- compiles clean,
//     prints `0` here instead of `42`, and is caught by **this fixture and
//     nothing else in the workspace**. That is what this fixture is for.
//   - A `nova-mir` lowering bug in `Builtin::TaskOutput` -- loading
//     `STATE_SLOT_TAG` instead of `STATE_SLOT_OUTPUT` -- also compiles clean
//     and also prints `0` here, but 18 tests in `nova-cli::run_tests` fail
//     under it. `block_on` and `join` read every value through that lowering,
//     so a bug in it is caught everywhere; this fixture is not what stands
//     between the project and it.
//   - A third claim once stood here: `task_timeout_future`'s Rust-side
//     signature widened to return `Future<Param(0)>`. It does not compile
//     either, blocked by the same rigid-`Param` rule described above.
//     Removed rather than left as a fourth guess.
async fn answer() -> Int {
    42
}

async fn main() {
    match timeout(Duration::from_secs(30), answer()).await {
        Ok(v) => println("${v}")
        Err(_) => println("elapsed")
    }
}
